You track an agent's reliability θ (the probability that an interaction with it goes well) with a Beta(1, 1) prior, one interaction per round. Its record, oldest first:
Round 1: good
Round 2: bad
Round 3: good
Round 4: good
Round 5: good
Round 6: good
Round 7: bad
Round 8: good
Round 9: bad
Round 10: bad
You use exponential forgetting with λ = 0.85: an interaction k rounds before the most recent one carries weight λ^k, so the most recent interaction has weight 1. With P the total weight of good interactions and N the total weight of bad ones, the posterior mean of θ is (P + 1)/(P + N + 2); the prior's pseudo-counts do not decay. What is the posterior mean of θ after round 10? Give it rounded to 4 decimal places. Reply with only the number.